3b. HOW SOFT AND HOW LUMINOUS WOULD THE X-RAYS HAVE TO BE?
    The literature quotes an X-ray share of up to ~10 % of the
    ionizations (McQuinn 2012 and refs. therein).  Scanning the two
    parameters that matter -- the low-energy cutoff of the escaping
    X-ray SED and L_X/SFR -- reproduces where that comes from.
    E_min[keV] Gamma    W_eff [eV]     N/Msun         rel. to UV
    0.20       1.5      121.4          1.623e+57      3.806e-03
    0.20       2.0      70.9           2.778e+57      6.515e-03
    0.20       2.5      49.68          3.965e+57      9.298e-03
    0.50       1.5      176.3          1.117e+57      2.621e-03
    0.50       2.0      109.9          1.792e+57      4.203e-03
    0.50       2.5      76.9           2.561e+57      6.007e-03
    1.00       1.5      379.6          5.189e+56      1.217e-03
    1.00       2.0      260.9          7.549e+56      1.770e-03
    1.00       2.5      191.2          1.03e+57       2.416e-03
    Even the most favourable corner (E_min = 0.2 keV, Gamma = 2.5,
    L_X/SFR = 1e40) stays at the 1e-2 level relative to escaping UV.
    Reaching ~10 % requires either f_esc(UV) ~ 0.01 or L_X/SFR ~
    1e42, both outside the observationally allowed range -- which
    is precisely the argument of McQuinn (2012).

4.  CROSS-CHECK -- the UV budget alone must close reionization
    Ionizing photons needed per H atom: 1 + N_rec ~ 2 (clumping)
    n_H (comoving) = 1.913e-07 cm^-3
    -> rho_* needed = 2.585e+07 Msun/Mpc^3 (comoving) with f_esc = 0.10
    -> Omega_* = 2.035e-04
    Observed z~6 stellar mass density is ~1e7 Msun/Mpc^3
    (Madau & Dickinson 2014, Fig. 11): the UV budget closes to
    within a factor of a few, which is the well-known 'photon-
    starved' character of reionization (Bolton & Haehnelt 2007).
    There is therefore no room for an additional channel at more
    than the tens-of-per-cent level, quite apart from its W.
